%I A400443 #21 Oct 02 2026 20:38:09
%S A400443 9,25,28,49,50,64,76,81,99,100,121,126,136,148,169,176,225,243,244,
%T A400443 289,325,343,344,361,364,376,441,508,513,529,540,576,625,676,726,729,
%U A400443 736,784,801,833,841,846,847,848,868,961,981,1000,1025,1084,1089,1176,1184
%N A400443 Numbers k such that k is an automorphic number in at least one base b in the range [2, k-2].
%C A400443 An automorphic number in base b is a natural number n whose base-b representation matches the terminal digits of its square n^2. Formally, n^2 == n (mod b^k), where k is the number of digits of n in base b, given by k = floor(log_b(n)) + 1. This condition is equivalent to requiring that b^k divides n*(n-1).
%C A400443 This sequence restricts the search of valid bases b to the nontrivial range 2 <= b <= n-2. The bases b = n-1 (where n == 1 mod b) and b >= n (where n has 1 digit and n^2 == n mod b requires n^2 - n divisible by b) are excluded to prevent all natural numbers from trivially satisfying the property.
%C A400443 It can be proved that the sequence is infinite: for any odd prime p, the number n = p^2 is always an element since it is automorphic in base b = 2p. For n = p^2 and b = 2p, the number of digits k is exactly 2, and the modular condition reduces to 4 dividing p^2 - 1, which is always true for any odd prime. As p goes to infinity, the asymptotic density of this sequence approaches 0.
%D A400443 C. F. Gauss, Disquisitiones Arithmeticae, Yale University Press, 1966.
%H A400443 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/M%C3%B6bius_function">Möbius function</a>
%e A400443 a(1) = 9 is in the sequence because in base b = 6, 9 is written as (13)_6 (k = 2 digits). Its square is 81, which in base 6 is written as (213)_6. The last 2 digits match (13)_6. Alternatively, b^k = 6^2 = 36, which divides 9*(9-1) = 72.
%e A400443 a(2) = 25 is in the sequence because in base b = 10, 25^2 = 625, which ends in 25. Here b^k = 10^2 = 100, which divides 25*24 = 600.
%t A400443 Select[Range[4, 1000], Function[n, AnyTrue[Range[2, n - 2], Function[b, Mod[n^2 - n, b^(IntegerLength[n, b])] == 0]]]]
%o A400443 (Python)
%o A400443 def isok(n):
%o A400443     if n < 4:
%o A400443         return False
%o A400443     for b in range(2, n - 1):
%o A400443         k = 0
%o A400443         temp = n
%o A400443         while temp > 0:
%o A400443             k += 1
%o A400443             temp //= b
%o A400443         if (n * (n - 1)) % (b ** k) == 0:
%o A400443             return True
%o A400443     return False
%o A400443 (PARI) isokv(v, v2) = for (i=1, #v, if (v[i] != v2[i], return(0));); 1;
%o A400443 isok(k) = for(b=2, k-2, my(v=Vecrev(digits(k, b)), v2=Vecrev(digits(k^2,b))); if (isokv(v, v2), return(b));); \\ _Michel Marcus_, Sep 26 2026
